<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Maritime Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">United States Military Government of the Philippine Islands</h1><p class="st-meta">1898 CE – 1902 CE · capital Manila</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">American army rule of the Philippines after the Spanish-American War, the regime that fought the Philippine-American War.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-states">United States</a> forces captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manila">Manila</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> on 13 August 1898, excluding Filipino revolutionaries from the city. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-paris-1898">Treaty of Paris</a> of December 1898 Spain ceded the islands for 20 million dollars, and President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-mckinley">William McKinley</a> proclaimed a policy of Benevolent Assimilation, governing through a military governor.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Military governors, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wesley-merritt">Wesley Merritt</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elwell-otis">Elwell Otis</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arthur-macarthur-jr">Arthur MacArthur</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adna-chaffee">Adna Chaffee</a>, held executive and legislative power. From February 1899 the army fought the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/first-philippine-republic">First Philippine Republic</a> in a brutal war marked by village burnings, reconcentration camps and heavy civilian deaths from disease and famine. At the same time it set up municipal governments, schools and courts.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>A civilian <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/insular-government-of-the-philippine-islands">Insular Government of the Philippine Islands</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-howard-taft">William Howard Taft</a> took over most functions in July 1901. On 4 July 1902 the war was declared over and military government formally ended, though fighting continued in places, especially in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moro-people">Moro</a> areas.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/united-states-military-government-of-the-philippine-islands.jpg" alt="United States Military Government of the Philippine Islands" width="320" height="169"><figcaption>Image: No machine-readable author provided. Jacobolus assumed (based on copyright claims). · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_United_States_(1896%E2%80%931908).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1898 CE – 1902 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Manila</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Elwell Otis, Arthur MacArthur, Adna Chaffee</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Roman Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>English, Spanish, Tagalog</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Philippines</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>US Military Government of the Philippines</dd></dl></aside></div>
